=== instances.create_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn create_instance(
also: api/src/app/instances.rs :: const MAX_CPUS ; api/src/app/instances.rs :: const MAX_MEMORY_GIB ; api/src/app/instances.rs :: const MIN_BOOT_DISK_GIB ; api/src/app/instances.rs :: const MAX_BOOT_DISK_GIB
doc: Creates an instance with its boot disk and an address, and records it as "creating". Nothing is saved unless every check passes. The instance gets a place on the rack a few seconds later, when it is next looked at.
---
def create_instance(caller, project_name, new):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find the project; collaborators and admins -> project_roles.authorize_project | ProjectNotFound | Forbidden
        project, _ = project_roles.authorize_project(
            caller, project_name, "collaborator")
        #> Check the name and the sizes -> names.validate_name | InvalidRequest
        names.validate_name(new.name)
        if not (1 <= new.ncpus <= 8
                and 1 <= new.memory_gib <= 32
                and 10 <= new.boot_disk_size_gib <= 200):
            raise ApiError.InvalidRequest          # 400
        #> Refuse a name already used in the project -> instance_store.instance_by_name | InstanceNameTaken | DiskNameTaken
        if instance_store.instance_by_name(project, new.name):
            raise ApiError.InstanceNameTaken       # 409
        # the boot disk is named "<instance name>-boot"
        if disk_store.disk_by_name(project, boot_disk_name):
            raise ApiError.DiskNameTaken           # 409
        #> Check the project's quota -> quotas.check_quota | QuotaExceeded
        quotas.check_quota(project, new.ncpus,
            new.memory_gib, new.boot_disk_size_gib)
        #> Take an address from the pool -> rack_store.address_allocate | AddressPoolExhausted
        address = rack_store.address_allocate(instance_id)
        if address is None:
            raise ApiError.AddressPoolExhausted    # 503
        #> Create the boot disk, attached -> disk_store.disk_insert
        boot_disk = disk_store.disk_insert(
            project, boot_disk_name,
            new.boot_disk_size_gib, instance_id)
        #> Record the instance as creating -> instance_store.instance_insert
        instance = Instance(new, project, boot_disk, address,
            state: "creating", state_changed_at: now)
        instance_store.instance_insert(instance)
        return instance

=== instances.settle_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn settle_instance(
also: api/src/app/instances.rs :: const SETTLE_SECONDS
doc: Finishes a change of state that has had its three seconds. Nothing runs in the background: an instance moves on only when someone looks at it, starts, stops or deletes it, or attaches a disk.
---
def settle_instance(instance, now):
    #> Leave it if its change is under three seconds old
    if now < instance.state_changed_at + 3 seconds:
        return instance
    #> Place a creating or starting instance -> placement.place_instance
    if instance.state in ["creating", "starting"]:
        # becomes running, or failed if the rack is full
        return placement.place_instance(instance, now)
    #> A stopping instance becomes stopped -> instance_store.instance_set_state
    if instance.state == "stopping":
        return instance_store.instance_set_state(
            instance, "stopped", now)
    return instance

=== instances.lookup_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn lookup_instance(
doc: Finds an instance by id, checks the caller's role on its project, and brings its state up to date. Someone with no role on the project is told the instance does not exist.
---
def lookup_instance(caller, instance_id, needed):
    #> Find the instance -> instance_store.instance_by_id | InstanceNotFound
    instance = instance_store.instance_by_id(instance_id)
    if instance is None:
        raise ApiError.InstanceNotFound            # 404
    #> Check the caller's role on its project -> project_roles.require_role | InstanceNotFound | Forbidden
    # no role at all reads as InstanceNotFound
    project_roles.require_role(
        caller, instance.project_id, needed)
    #> Bring its state up to date -> settle_instance
    return settle_instance(instance, now)

=== instances.list_instances
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn list_instances(
doc: The instances of a project, sorted by name, each with its state brought up to date.
---
def list_instances(caller, project_name):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find the project; any role may look -> project_roles.authorize_project | ProjectNotFound
        project, _ = project_roles.authorize_project(
            caller, project_name, "viewer")
        #> Load its instances -> instance_store.instances_in_project
        found = instance_store.instances_in_project(
            project.id)
        #> Bring each one's state up to date -> settle_instance
        return [settle_instance(each, now) for each in found]

=== instances.view_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn view_instance(
doc: One instance as it is now. Any role on its project is enough.
---
def view_instance(caller, instance_id):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find it; any role may look -> lookup_instance | InstanceNotFound
        return lookup_instance(caller, instance_id, "viewer")

=== instances.start_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn start_instance(
doc: Starts a stopped or failed instance. Asking again while it is starting or running changes nothing. An instance that is being created or is stopping must finish first.
---
def start_instance(caller, instance_id):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find it; collaborators and admins -> lookup_instance | InstanceNotFound | Forbidden
        instance = lookup_instance(
            caller, instance_id, "collaborator")
        #> Leave a running or starting instance alone
        if instance.state in ["running", "starting"]:
            return instance
        #> Mark a stopped or failed instance as starting -> instance_store.instance_set_state
        if instance.state in ["stopped", "failed"]:
            # an earlier failure reason is cleared
            return instance_store.instance_set_state(
                instance, "starting", now)
        #> Refuse while it is creating or stopping | InstanceBusy
        raise ApiError.InstanceBusy                # 409

=== instances.stop_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn stop_instance(
doc: Stops a running instance. Asking again while it is stopping or stopped changes nothing. An instance that is being created, is starting or has failed cannot be stopped.
---
def stop_instance(caller, instance_id):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find it; collaborators and admins -> lookup_instance | InstanceNotFound | Forbidden
        instance = lookup_instance(
            caller, instance_id, "collaborator")
        #> Leave a stopped or stopping instance alone
        if instance.state in ["stopped", "stopping"]:
            return instance
        #> Mark a running instance as stopping -> instance_store.instance_set_state
        if instance.state == "running":
            # it keeps its place on the rack until stopped
            return instance_store.instance_set_state(
                instance, "stopping", now)
        #> Refuse while creating, starting or failed | InstanceNotRunning
        raise ApiError.InstanceNotRunning          # 409

=== instances.delete_instance
kind: def
src: api/src/app/instances.rs :: pub fn delete_instance(
doc: Deletes a stopped or failed instance. Its boot disk goes with it, its other disks are detached and kept, and its address returns to the pool.
---
def delete_instance(caller, instance_id):
    with store_lock:
        #> Find it; collaborators and admins -> lookup_instance | InstanceNotFound | Forbidden
        instance = lookup_instance(
            caller, instance_id, "collaborator")
        #> Refuse unless it is stopped or failed | InstanceNotStopped
        if instance.state not in ["stopped", "failed"]:
            raise ApiError.InstanceNotStopped      # 409
        #> Delete its boot disk and detach the rest -> disk_store.disks_attached_to
        for disk in disk_store.disks_attached_to(instance.id):
            if disk.is_boot_disk:
                disk_store.disk_delete(disk.id)
            else:
                disk_store.disk_set_attachment(disk.id, None)
        #> Return its address to the pool -> rack_store.address_release
        rack_store.address_release(instance.id)
        #> Delete the instance -> instance_store.instance_delete
        instance_store.instance_delete(instance.id)

=== instance_store.instance_set_state
kind: def
src: api/src/db/instance_store.rs :: pub fn instance_set_state(
doc: Moves an instance to a new state and notes when. The reason for a failure is saved with it, and cleared by any later change.
---
def instance_set_state(
        instance_id, state, failure_reason, now):
    instance.state = state
    instance.state_changed_at = now
    instance.failure_reason = failure_reason
    return instance

=== Creating an instance from the console
kind: flow
domain: Instances
doc: What happens between submitting the create-instance form and seeing the instance running.
---
#> The person submits the form -> instance_actions.submit_create_instance
They choose a name, a number of CPUs, an amount of memory and the size of the boot disk. The console checks the name and sizes, and that the project has enough of its quota left, before sending anything.
#> The API checks who is asking -> POST /v1/instances
The person must be signed in and be a collaborator or an admin of the project. A viewer is refused.
#> The API checks the request -> instances.create_instance
It checks the name and sizes again, that no instance or boot disk in the project has the name, and that the project's quota has room. Stopped and failed instances still count against the quota.
#> The instance gets an address and a boot disk -> rack_store.address_allocate
One address is taken from the rack's pool and a boot disk is created, attached to the instance. If no address is free the request is refused and nothing is created.
#> The instance is recorded as creating
The API answers at once. The console shows the instance as "creating".
#> The console waits -> instance_actions.wait_for_instance
It asks for the instance once a second, for up to 30 seconds, and stops when it is running or failed.
#> The rack finds room, or does not -> placement.place_instance
Three seconds after it was created, the instance is given a place if the rack has the CPUs and memory free, and becomes running. If the rack is full it becomes failed, with a reason the console shows.
#> The person sees the outcome
The console reloads the project so the usage is right, then says the instance is running at its address, or that it failed and what to try. A failed instance keeps its disk, address and quota, and can be started again or deleted.

=== Stopping an instance
kind: flow
domain: Instances
doc: What happens when a person stops a running instance.
---
#> The person presses Stop and confirms -> instance_actions.stop_instance
The console offers this only for a running instance, and asks first because whatever runs on the instance is interrupted.
#> The API checks who is asking and the state -> POST /v1/instances/{instance}/stop
A collaborator or an admin of the project may stop it. Only a running instance can be stopped. Stopping one that is already stopping or stopped changes nothing, and one that is being created, is starting or has failed is refused.
#> The instance is marked as stopping -> instances.stop_instance
It still holds its place on the rack while it stops.
#> The console waits -> instance_actions.wait_for_instance
It asks for the instance once a second until it is stopped.
#> The instance becomes stopped -> instances.settle_instance
Three seconds later it is stopped, and its CPUs and memory on the rack are free for others. It keeps its disks, its address and its share of the project's quota.
